WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.050
LORIN PRICE: My
name is Lorin Price.

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:04.390
I'm a networking specialist
customer engineer

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:05.350
at Google Cloud.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:07.780
And today, I'm going to talk
to you about the networking

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:10.630
concepts for Cloud SQL
that are helpful to know

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:13.840
for implementation,
security, and connectivity.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.980
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:22.180
Cloud SQL provides a managed
service for MySQL, PostgreSQL,

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.370
and SQL Server databases.

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:27.400
Cloud SQL offers many
services like backups,

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.070
high availability,
maintenance, and more,

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:32.590
and all managed by Google Cloud.

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:35.770
Each Cloud SQL instance is
powered by a virtual machine

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:38.110
running in a Google-managed
environment that

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:41.380
is connected to the environment
that you own and manage.

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.820
By running in a
Google-managed environment,

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.750
this allows Cloud
SQL to abstract away

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:50.110
all of the management overhead
to be offloaded from you,

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:52.420
the user, to Google Cloud.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:56.020
Even though Cloud SQL sits in
a Google-managed environment,

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.780
Google will never have access
to the data that resides

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:01.660
on the Cloud SQL instance.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.599
Cloud SQL can be deployed with
two different access patterns,

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:06.700
public IP and private IP.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.430
Let's start by taking a look
into the public IP deployment

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:10.360
pattern.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:13.360
A public IP is an
IPv4 address that

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.960
can receive connections from
devices both inside and outside

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.840
of Google's network, including
from locations like your home

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.050
or office.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:22.900
Let's take a look at
the access patterns

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.520
from different scenarios.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.400
Let's say you want to connect
from a client on prem.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.780
To connect to a
Cloud SQL instance,

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.230
the traffic will egress
from your on-prem network

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.690
and access Cloud SQL
through a public IP

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:36.460
address on the internet.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.740
If a client is located
in Google Cloud,

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.710
the traffic will egress through
an instance with a public IP

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:45.490
address or a Cloud NAT and
ingress to the Cloud SQL

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:48.500
instance through its
public IP address.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.040
If the client is located
somewhere on the internet,

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.130
that client can access the
Cloud SQL instance directly

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:55.690
through its public IP.

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:58.810
Any clients making a connection
to a Cloud SQL instance

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.390
hosted with a public
IP must be allow

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:03.940
listed through the use
of authorized networks.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.250
By default, the
allow list is empty,

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.430
and nothing can make connections
to the Cloud SQL instance.

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:12.190
Clients can also connect
to a Cloud SQL instance

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.830
hosted with a public IP
through a Cloud SQL auth

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:18.130
proxy, which we'll discuss
more later in this video.

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:21.400
The other Cloud SQL deployment
pattern is through private IP.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.330
A private IP is an IPv4 that's
accessible on a Virtual Private

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:27.130
Cloud, or VPC.

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.320
Configuring your instance
with a private IP

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.810
is preferred when connecting
from a client with access

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.980
to a VPC, either within the
VPC or connected to the VPC

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.960
through a hybrid connection.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:41.890
Private IP Cloud SQL is deployed
using Private Service Access.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.710
Private Service Access is
a commonly used private

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:46.870
routing method on
Google Cloud that

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:49.450
allows workloads in
your VPC to access

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.570
Google-managed infrastructure
services privately.

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:54.640
Private Service
Access is implemented

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.150
as a VPC peering connection
between your VPC network

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:01.390
and the underlying Google Cloud
VPC network where your Cloud

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:03.700
SQL instance resides.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:06.400
There are two parts to
the Private Service Access

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:09.100
configuration process.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.320
You will start by
selecting an existing

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:15.130
or allocating a new IP address
range as the Private Service

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:16.940
Access range.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.240
You also have the option
of allowing Google

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.580
to allocate the range for you.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:24.970
In this case, Google will
automatically allocate an IP

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:31.180
range of prefix length /20 and
use the name default IP range.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:34.300
If you're going to create
instances in multiple regions

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:38.140
for different database types,
a /20 or larger range is

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.660
recommended.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:44.830
Next, a private connection,
otherwise known as VPC peer,

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:47.050
will be created from
your VPC network

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:50.380
to the underlying
service producer network.

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:53.260
Keep in mind that you only need
to configure Private Service

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.890
Access once for Cloud
SQL per VPC network.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:00.310
And the IP range you allocate
for Private Service Access will

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:03.070
become available for use
by all Google services

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:06.370
globally that support
Private Service Access.

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:08.950
Let's talk through
the access patterns.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:11.620
Let's start with a client
attempting to access the Cloud

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.810
SQL instance from the internet.

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:16.839
Because Cloud SQL is
deployed on a private IP,

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:18.700
there's no way
for public clients

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:21.170
to access the instance directly.

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:22.690
Now let's walk
through a connection

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:25.870
initiated from within
the VPC and Google Cloud.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:27.700
Clients are able
to connect directly

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:31.277
to the Cloud SQL instance
from the directly peered VPC.

00:04:31.277 --> 00:04:35.710
Additionally, you can also
use the Cloud SQL auth proxy.

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:38.590
If you are in a peered hub
and spoke architecture model,

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:41.170
and you're trying to make a
connection from a VPC that

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:44.710
is not directly paired to the
Private Service Access VPC,

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:47.620
you must use a proxy
due to the nontransitive

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.470
routing nature of Google's VPCs.

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:52.720
Be aware you can also
replace a peering hub

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.000
and spoke architecture
with a VPN hub

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.030
and spoke model, so that the
Private Service Access route

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:00.610
can be advertised over the VPN.

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:03.355
Finally, for a client
to connect from on prem,

00:05:03.355 --> 00:05:05.980
you'll need to make sure you're
advertising the Private Service

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:09.250
Access route over the
VPN or the Interconnect.

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:13.150
If the VLAN or VPN lands in
the VPC that is peered directly

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:15.370
with the Private
Service Access VPC,

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:17.770
then you'll be able
to connect directly.

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:21.670
If the VLAN or VPN lands
in another peered VPC, then

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:24.010
you'll need to use
a proxy to connect.

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:27.850
Remember, you'll never be able
to ping a Cloud SQL instance.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.770
You'll only be able to
initiate connections

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:32.350
through applications,
the respective database

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:35.892
client, or the Cloud SQL proxy.

00:05:35.892 --> 00:05:37.350
Let's quickly touch
on some network

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.060
security-related topics.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:42.000
For Cloud SQL deployed
with public IP,

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.280
you can use authorized
networks to determine

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.320
a list of whitelisted
IP addresses

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:49.060
that can access your instance.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:52.170
You cannot use firewall rules to
control ingress to a Cloud SQL

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:53.710
instance.

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:55.920
You can use the
Cloud SQL auth proxy

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.150
to encrypt all traffic to and
from the database using TLS

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:03.270
and to control who and what
can connect to your Cloud SQL

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:04.930
instance.

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:07.380
So let's summarize the three
most important takeaways

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:08.790
from today.

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:11.460
Cloud SQL can be deployed
with either a public IP

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.560
or private IP access pattern.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:16.170
If you want to use
Cloud SQL private IP,

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:19.410
you must create a Private
Service Access IP range,

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.450
and a VPC peer will be created.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:24.090
Remember this when
it comes to routing.

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:27.180
You can use authorized networks
or Cloud SQL auth proxy

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:31.140
to determine who and what can
access the Cloud SQL instance.

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:32.640
Thanks for watching,
and look out

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:35.010
for another video on the
concepts of networking

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:36.870
for Google Cloud's
managed services.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:40.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

